## AUTHORIZATION DISCIPLINE

### User-Led Changes

Execute code edits only when the user explicitly directs the modification. Refrain from speculative refactors or cleanups.

- **Do not:** Adjust `src/utils/time.ts` because "it looked better this way."
- **Acceptable:** Update `src/utils/time.ts` when the user accepts, approves or directly commands you to code something related to that file.

### Permission Discipline

Each coding action requires explicit, current user permission. Previous permissions do not carry over.

- **Do not:** Proceed with coding after receiving "YOU CAN CODE NOW" then later being told to stop, then proceeding again when user says "Ok, implement the tests"
- **Acceptable:** Wait for explicit permission each time before starting any coding action

### Error Accountability

All type errors in the codebase are your responsibility to fix, regardless of whether you caused them.

- **Do not:** Commit with pre-existing type errors, claiming "those aren't from my changes"
- **Acceptable:** Fix all type errors encountered, even if they originated from previous code

### Comment Restraint

Add comments solely when the code demands clarification; keep the tone technical and impersonal.

- **Do not:** `// I changed this because you asked for it`
- **Do not:** `// apps/web/src/components/MyComponent.tsx` (file path comments)
- **Acceptable:** `// Explains edge case for daylight-saving transition`

### Document Framework Quirks

When solving non-obvious framework behaviors or working around library limitations, add inline comments explaining both the problem and why the solution works.

- **Trigger:** Framework behaves counterintuitively (e.g., D3's `scale.ticks(n)` treats `n` as hint, not requirement)
- **Format:** Brief problem description + why this solution addresses it
- **Example:** `// D3's scale.ticks(n) treats count as hint, preferring calendar-friendly intervals. Use explicit time intervals for deterministic results.`

